WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:09.288
This is Chapter 3:
prevention through audit.

4
00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:13.634
A setting in a policy protects
nothing until the device enforces it,

5
00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:18.137
so the check is on the device, against
the stage the ring is meant to be at.

6
00:00:18.137 --> 00:00:22.760
We'll start from Learn's three device
commands and the guide's event query,

7
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.459
read how the kit compares every
device with its ring's plan,

8
00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:29.387
and walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:32.255
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved;

10
00:00:32.255 --> 00:00:35.960
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.135
Why check on the device?

12
00:00:41.135 --> 00:00:45.635
Because a value is only right or wrong
against the stage its ring is at.

13
00:00:45.635 --> 00:00:50.659
Network protection reading two, audit, is
exactly right on a ring still auditing,

14
00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:53.323
and drift on a ring that
should be blocking.

15
00:00:53.323 --> 00:00:56.210
By the end of this chapter
you can say, ring by ring,

16
00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.964
whether devices enforce the
prevention settings at the stage

17
00:00:59.964 --> 00:01:03.285
the change record set, and list
every device that doesn't.

18
00:01:03.285 --> 00:01:06.994
It's for the developers, the
platform owner who owns this gate,

19
00:01:06.994 --> 00:01:11.297
and the security operations center,
which reads the events every week.

20
00:01:11.297 --> 00:01:15.095
The setting values themselves live
in the Devices book's policies;

21
00:01:15.095 --> 00:01:16.940
this chapter checks them.

22
00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.103
Here's what Learn gives you.

23
00:01:22.103 --> 00:01:27.052
Get M P computer status shows tamper
and real-time protection, true when on.

24
00:01:27.052 --> 00:01:32.108
Get M P preference shows network
protection, zero off, one block, two audit;

25
00:01:32.108 --> 00:01:34.383
the two Windows Server settings;

26
00:01:34.383 --> 00:01:38.427
and controlled folder access,
with its disk-only modes too.

27
00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:43.691
Get net firewall profile, from the active
store, shows the firewall as enforced.

28
00:01:43.691 --> 00:01:46.390
And the guide's query
counts the audit and block

29
00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.090
events and tampering
attempts over seven days.

30
00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:55.314
Learn adds three conditions: network
protection needs the antivirus in active mode,

31
00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:59.315
needs its own setting on
Windows Server 2019 and later,

32
00:01:59.315 --> 00:02:05.990
and needs datagram processing off on domain
controllers, DNS, file and SQL servers.

33
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.790
Run by hand, those commands
leave a lot to you.

34
00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.720
Someone has to know what
each ring should read,

35
00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.190
so the agency's prevention
plan names each ring's stage.

36
00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:22.395
A bare two reads as fine on
a ring that should block,

37
00:02:22.395 --> 00:02:25.990
so the kit prints expected
and actual as number and name.

38
00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.098
A file server looks like any other,

39
00:02:28.098 --> 00:02:32.390
so the inventory's role column
decides where the datagram check runs.

40
00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.779
Passive mode silently
disables network protection,

41
00:02:35.779 --> 00:02:38.090
so the antivirus mode must be normal.

42
00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.340
The fleet query gives counts, so
the drift list names devices.

43
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:46.065
And a device nobody collected would
look compliant, so it's listed.

44
00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:50.190
The verdict: Learn's commands
collect; the kit compares.

45
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.365
For each in-scope device,
the kit runs these checks.

46
00:02:57.365 --> 00:02:59.602
Real-time and tamper protection on.

47
00:02:59.602 --> 00:03:02.190
The antivirus in normal, active mode.

48
00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:05.458
Network protection and
controlled folder access

49
00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:08.415
at the ring's stage from
the plan, block or audit.

50
00:03:08.415 --> 00:03:12.164
On servers, network protection
allowed on the server;

51
00:03:12.164 --> 00:03:15.402
and on high-UDP roles,
datagram processing off.

52
00:03:15.402 --> 00:03:18.889
And each firewall profile
on, with inbound blocked.

53
00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:21.209
It runs the guide's event query too,

54
00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:25.515
and writes the device checks, the
drift, the events and a summary.

55
00:03:25.515 --> 00:03:29.091
Moving a ring from audit to block
is a change to the plan file,

56
00:03:29.091 --> 00:03:32.740
under the same change record
that changes the Intune policy.

57
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.160
The kit never changes a policy.

58
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:39.960
Who's involved?

59
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.591
The platform owner owns this gate,
moves each ring under a change record,

60
00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.260
and reviews the exclusions each quarter.

61
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.509
The security operations
center reviews blocks and

62
00:03:50.509 --> 00:03:54.060
tampering attempts every week,
alongside the drift list.

63
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.989
The operator collects a
sample of device states

64
00:03:56.989 --> 00:03:59.698
per ring per site and
works the drift tickets.

65
00:03:59.698 --> 00:04:03.509
One caution: the fixtures
are eleven fictional devices.

66
00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.262
On a real fleet, sample per ring per site,

67
00:04:06.262 --> 00:04:09.273
as the guide's post-change
validation says.

68
00:04:09.273 --> 00:04:13.000
The coverage check in Chapter
2 is the fleet-wide view.

69
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:18.850
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

70
00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.113
Step 1 reads the plan the
devices are checked against.

71
00:04:22.113 --> 00:04:27.899
Rings 0 and 1 are in block; rings 2,
3 and the servers are still auditing.

72
00:04:27.899 --> 00:04:31.813
Step 2 runs the prevention
check, and finds three drifts.

73
00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:34.826
One device in ring 1 has
tamper protection off.

74
00:04:34.826 --> 00:04:39.387
One in ring 2 has network protection
off, though its ring should be auditing.

75
00:04:39.387 --> 00:04:42.512
And the file server has
datagram processing on,

76
00:04:42.512 --> 00:04:45.476
which Learn says to turn
off on high-UDP roles.

77
00:04:45.476 --> 00:04:48.385
Three inventory devices
have no state file:

78
00:04:48.385 --> 00:04:51.900
the two never onboarded,
and the excepted kiosk.

79
00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.975
Step 3 reads every check
on the file server.

80
00:04:58.975 --> 00:05:02.597
A server gets the two server
checks the workstations don't:

81
00:05:02.597 --> 00:05:06.068
network protection allowed
on the server, which passes,

82
00:05:06.068 --> 00:05:09.463
and datagram processing for
its file role, which fails.

83
00:05:09.463 --> 00:05:11.342
Step 4 reads the week's events:

84
00:05:11.342 --> 00:05:15.962
network protection audited twelve
times on four devices, three blocks,

85
00:05:15.962 --> 00:05:20.738
controlled folder access audited and
blocked, and one tampering attempt.

86
00:05:20.738 --> 00:05:24.150
The security operations center
reads that with the drift list.

87
00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:27.267
A device with tamper
protection off and a tampering

88
00:05:27.267 --> 00:05:30.680
attempt in the same week is
an incident, not a ticket.

89
00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:38.630
Lab 2 uses a ring 0 test device in
a test tenant, never production,

90
00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.668
with the policies assigned and
network protection in audit.

91
00:05:42.668 --> 00:05:45.767
On the device, collect
its state, elevated.

92
00:05:45.767 --> 00:05:49.617
On your workstation, sign in and
run the prevention check live.

93
00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:52.723
Then test network protection
the way Learn does:

94
00:05:52.723 --> 00:05:55.905
in a browser other than
Edge, open Learn's test site.

95
00:05:55.905 --> 00:06:00.275
In audit mode the site opens
and event 1125 is logged;

96
00:06:00.275 --> 00:06:03.730
in block mode it's
blocked, with event 1126.

97
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:08.418
Run the check again the next day, and the
audited event appears in the events file.

98
00:06:08.418 --> 00:06:10.630
Nothing changed on the device.

99
00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:18.092
Here's what the prevention check
proves, against NIST's catalog.

100
00:06:18.092 --> 00:06:20.679
Antivirus and real-time protection on,

101
00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:24.155
in active mode, supports
malicious code protection.

102
00:06:24.155 --> 00:06:28.543
The host firewall on with inbound
blocked supports boundary protection.

103
00:06:28.543 --> 00:06:31.574
Network protection and
controlled folder access at

104
00:06:31.574 --> 00:06:34.468
the ring's stage supports
configuration settings.

105
00:06:34.468 --> 00:06:38.086
And tamper protection on, with
tampering attempts counted,

106
00:06:38.086 --> 00:06:40.967
supports software and
information integrity.

107
00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:44.155
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
as the guide maps them.

108
00:06:44.155 --> 00:06:48.330
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

109
00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.480
What breaks in production?

110
00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.584
A ring moved to block in
Intune but not in the plan:

111
00:06:56.584 --> 00:07:01.318
every device reports drift, so change
the plan with the same change record.

112
00:07:01.318 --> 00:07:03.733
Defender Antivirus in passive mode:

113
00:07:03.733 --> 00:07:08.242
network protection doesn't run; the
antivirus mode check catches it.

114
00:07:08.242 --> 00:07:11.034
Datagram processing on
a domain controller,

115
00:07:11.034 --> 00:07:14.580
the performance risk Learn
documents for high-UDP roles.

116
00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.793
Tamper protection turned off
locally, so settings drift silently.

117
00:07:18.793 --> 00:07:24.610
And a device with no state file, which looks
compliant by absence; the kit lists it.

118
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:29.710
Here's what done looks like.

119
00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:33.572
The prevention plan matches the
Intune policy for each ring.

120
00:07:33.572 --> 00:07:37.523
Every ring has at least one
sampled device state this week.

121
00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:40.578
Every drift row has a ticket,
and a tamper drift with a

122
00:07:40.578 --> 00:07:44.060
tampering attempt goes to the
security operations center.

123
00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:47.973
Network protection's test site
was blocked on a ring in block.

124
00:07:47.973 --> 00:07:50.923
And the run folder is
recorded as evidence.

125
00:07:50.923 --> 00:07:53.928
Next, Chapter 4 reads the
same device states for

126
00:07:53.928 --> 00:07:57.011
the attack surface reduction
rules, ring by ring.

127
00:07:57.011 --> 00:07:58.170
Thanks for watching.
